--- body ---
## Context

Tracker: 0001, but landing after it -- changing chunk geometry changes results,
which is exactly what "same corpus in, same results out" forbids while the
storage swap is being validated.

Three chunk geometries exist, none reachable from configuration:

| where | geometry | how set |
|---|---|---|
| metadata passages, `index-manager.ts:560` and `:875` | 512 / 64 overlap | bare `chunkText()` defaults |
| attachment full text, `index-manager.ts:890` | 1200 / 150 | `FULLTEXT_CHUNK_SIZE`, exported const |
| passage locators, `fulltext/passages.ts:72` | 800 / 100 | bare `chunkWithOffsets()` defaults |

`grep -n "chunk" src/config.ts` returns nothing. `chunkText` is a free
function called directly at three sites -- no interface, no injection point.

512 was tuned for a resident JS index, where every passage cost heap. On disk
that constraint is gone and larger chunks mean fewer rows and a smaller index,
but nobody can measure the tradeoff while the number is a default nobody can
reach.

Note the two geometries are not merely different, they disagree: a search hit
comes from `chunkText`, while the locator `zotero_get_fulltext` reports for it
comes from `chunkWithOffsets` at a different size and a different treatment of
whitespace.

## What Zotero 10 does and does not give us

Investigated on the author's own library (13 631 cached attachments,
`~/data/Zotero`), because the hunch was that Zotero extracts structure worth
chunking on.

**It is smart about extraction, and does not expose structure.**

- `.zotero-ft-info` is a `pdfinfo` dump -- `Pages`, `Title`, `Author`,
  `Subject`, `Keywords`, page size, PDF version. Present in 200 of 200 files
  sampled. The API surfaces the useful part of it: `/items/KEY/fulltext`
  returns `indexedPages` and `totalPages`, which `get-fulltext.ts:111` already
  reads. pdfjs re-extraction is the fallback for *unindexed* attachments only,
  not a duplicated page count -- that part of the design is sound.
- `.zotero-ft-cache` is a **flat text stream with line breaks and no page
  markers**. Form feeds appear in **5 of 300** caches sampled (1,7%). So there
  are no page boundaries to chunk on, and none of Zotero's own page knowledge
  reaches the text.
- No headings, no sections, no paragraph semantics beyond newlines. What
  zoteus displays as structure is invented downstream: `findSection` is a
  regex guess over preceding lines, and `approxPage` is linear interpolation
  (`ceil((charStart+1)/totalChars * totalPages)`) -- a proportional estimate
  presented in the same field as a real page number.

So chunking on document structure is not available from Zotero today. The
useful geometry question is the boring one: what size and overlap, measured.

## A second finding, recorded here rather than as its own ticket

**Zotero 10 already runs FTS5**, and its tokenizer choices are worth reading
against 0002's:

    fulltextContent     fts5(text, tokenize='unicode61', content='', contentless_delete=1)
    fulltextContentCJK  fts5(text, tokenize='ascii',     content='', contentless_delete=1)
    fulltextNotes       fts5(text, tokenize='trigram',   content='', contentless_delete=1)

Two consequences.

`content=''` makes those tables **contentless**: they can be MATCHed but the
text cannot be read back. That is why zoteus reads the cache rather than
querying Zotero's index, and it forecloses the obvious "just reuse Zotero's
FTS5" shortcut -- worth stating in the #10 writeup before someone proposes it.

Zotero maintains a **separate CJK table** because `unicode61` does not segment
CJK; it falls back to `ascii` there. 0002 chose
`unicode61 remove_diacritics 2` and inherits the same blind spot: a Chinese or
Japanese document indexes as very few enormous tokens. Below the severity floor
for this library (French and English), so it is recorded, not ticketed -- but
it is a real limitation of the tokenizer decision and should be named rather
than discovered.

## Actions

1. `ZOTEUS_INDEX_CHUNK_SIZE` / `ZOTEUS_INDEX_CHUNK_OVERLAP` and the full-text
   pair in `config.ts`, defaulting to today's exact values (512/64, 1200/150)
   so nothing changes by default.
2. Thread them from config to the three call sites. No strategy interface:
   a pluggable chunker is speculative until a second strategy exists, and
   YAGNI applies.
3. Decide deliberately whether `chunkWithOffsets` (800/100) follows the same
   settings or keeps its own -- it serves locators, not retrieval, and the two
   jobs may legitimately want different windows. Say which, and why, in the code.
4. Re-run `bench/fts5_bench.mjs` at two or three geometries and record index
   size, build time and query latency against passage count.

## Test

Red step: assert the default configuration reproduces today's passage count on
a fixture exactly. That is the whole safety property -- a config knob whose
default silently changes the index is worse than a constant.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Chunk size and overlap reachable from configuration, defaults unchanged
- [ ] Default-path passage count byte-identical to today on a fixture
- [x] `STATE.md`'s measurement table caption states that the 477 511 and
      408 628 passage counts are different corpora (metadata included or not,
      overlap or not), so the row is not read as like-for-like
- [ ] Size/overlap tradeoff measured and recorded in `bench/`
